<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Golkar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1964">1964</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Party of Functional Groups, Partai Golongan Karya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Golkar is an Indonesian political party that began in 1964 as an army-backed federation of functional groups opposed to the Communist Party. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s New Order it served as the regime's electoral vehicle, winning every parliamentary election from 1971 to 1997 with the support of the army and civil service. After Suharto's fall in 1998 it reinvented itself as an ordinary party and has remained one of the largest forces in Indonesian politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golkar.jpg" alt="Golkar" width="320" height="306" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sekber Golkar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logo_Golkar.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1959 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sukarno">Sukarno</a> introduced Guided Democracy, in which so-called functional groups would play a role in government in place of political parties. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-armed-forces">Indonesian National Armed Forces</a> supported them because they believed the groups would balance the growing strength of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communist-party-of-indonesia">Communist Party of Indonesia</a>. In 1960 Sukarno gave sectoral groups such as teachers, the armed forces and police, workers and artists seats in the Mutual Cooperation House of Representatives. The armed forces then founded an anti-communist trade union, Soksi, and used it as the core of the Joint Secretariat of Functional Groups, or Sekber Golkar. Its members included the Trikarya organisations and professional groups, and during the New Order it was formally divided into main organisation groups.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> was elected the second president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in March 1968. Having no party of his own, he settled on Golkar in order to distance himself from the old regime, and ordered his associate Ali Murtopo to turn it from a federation of organisations into an electoral machine. It presented itself as non-ideological, promising economic development and stability, and encouraged civil servants to vote for it as a sign of loyalty. Murtopo sometimes used the military and gangs of young men to eliminate political competition.</p><p>Golkar won 62 per cent in 1971 and then 62 per cent in 1977, 64 in 1982, 73 in 1987, 68 in 1992 and 74 in 1997. After 1973 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> banned all parties except the Indonesian Democratic Party and the United Development Party. Five of six New Order chairmen were military officers.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> fell in May 1998, Akbar Tandjung became chairman in July by beating Army General Edi Sudrajat, the first time a chairman was elected rather than appointed. In 1999 Golkar lost its first democratic election to Megawati Sukarnoputri's PDI-P, with 20 per cent of the vote, and then supported Abdurrahman Wahid for president. It won the 2004 legislative election with 21 per cent, and its member Jusuf Kalla became vice president that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Jusuf Kalla became chairman in December 2004, and Aburizal Bakrie in 2009. Several former members founded rival parties, among them Hanura under Wiranto and Gerindra under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prabowo-subianto">Prabowo Subianto</a>. A dispute between camps led by Bakrie and Agung Laksono ended on 17 May 2016 when Setya Novanto was elected chairman, and the party then backed President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joko-widodo">Joko Widodo</a>. Airlangga Hartarto later became chairman, and in 2024 Golkar won 102 seats in the House of Representatives. Its stated ideology has been described as centrist, centre-right or catch-all by observers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Golkar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its pillars were the army's dual function (dwifungsi) in defence and government, the regime party <b>Golkar</b>, a centralised bureaucracy, technocratic economic planning and tight control of the press.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
